Not likely; at least, not more useful answers that satisfy everybody.
The problem is that certain points in the Islamic calendar depend, if
you interpret the rules strictly, on when the new moon is first visible.
Not above the horizon; _visible_. This can get very awkward in countries
with a lot of clouds, and AFAIK in such countries one uses an "official"
visibility, e.g. the one in Mecca. Even so, for some kinds of Islam, the
occurance of some religious festivals and events cannot be determined in
advance; and, again AFAIK, Ramadan is one of those.
There is such a thing as a "theoretical date of Ramadan", but it tends
to be less accurate the further into the future you look. One could not
compile NetHack and expect its Ramadan prediction to be correct in ten
years' time.
